 shader's raster draw, else the tessellation evaluation shader, else the vertex
 shader. Each entry of a multi-draw is a Metal draw of its own, so every entry
-after the first restarts the encoder as a separate draw call would. Restarting reuses the existing
+after the first restarts the encoder as a separate draw call would. Every
+render encoder of a layered or multiview pass also starts a command buffer of
+its own. Restarting reuses the existing
 render-pass flush path, so the attachments are reloaded and nothing is lost.
 The cost is one attachment load and store per such draw; a pass that never
 writes the layer, or is not layered, is untouched. The
@@ -150,6 +154,19 @@ workaround, and ``kosmickrisp_layered_draw_multi_draw`` and
 through a geometry shader's layer: without the per-draw restart and the
 raster-stage check, each loses the same 908672 points.
 
+The restart does not remove the loss entirely. A restarted encoder still
+occasionally loses whole 1984-vertex batches from the start of its
+layer-selecting draw: about one isolated ``kosmickrisp_layered_draw_sequence``
+run in twenty loses 126976 or 253952 points of the second layer, and a
+standalone Metal 4 program without Mesa loses them the same way. The loss
+needs an encoder that draws into a different layer than the encoder before it,
+is ordered after that encoder by a queue barrier or a fence, and follows one
+that drew many primitives without clearing the attachment; a 16384-point draw
+after a 1048576-point one loses points in most fresh processes. A ``CLEAR``
+load op protects only the encoder right after it, and no other load op,
+barrier form, fence or extra encoder avoids the loss while keeping the
+encoders ordered, so no driver-side avoidance is known.
+
 Restarting an encoder mid-pass has to preserve everything the open encoder
 was carrying. Vertex amplification was the one piece that did not survive:
 ``vkCmdClearAttachments`` turns amplification off because the meta clear
@@ -162,6 +179,7 @@ encoder start, and ``kosmickrisp_multiview_clear_attachments`` covers it.
 
